                <head>138. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>On September 21, at the request of Greece, the General Committee of the General
                    Assembly opened discussion on the question of including the Cyprus issue on the
                    Assembly’s agenda. That day in his address before the committee, Ambassador
                        Lodge stated that inscription of the
                    Cyprus issue at this time would not contribute toward the peaceful settlement of
                    international disputes. The text of Lodge’s statement is in Department of State <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bulletin,</hi> October 3, 1955, pages 545–546.</p>
                <p>On September 23, the General Assembly, by a vote of 28 to 22 with 10 abstentions,
                    adopted the General Committee’s recommendation not to inscribe the Cyprus
                    question. Both the United States and the United Kingdom voted against
                    inscription.</p>
            </div>
